{
  "result": "At dawn, the city belongs to people who are usually invisible. Bakers lift warm trays from ovens, nurses step into buses with tired eyes, and street sweepers draw patient circles around last night\u2019s confetti. Windows brighten one by one, not dramatically, but with the quiet confidence of stars appearing in reverse.\n\nI like this hour because nothing seems finished. The day is still negotiable. A missed train can become a longer walk; a wrong turn can reveal a courtyard full of herbs; an unanswered message can remain, for another hour, a possibility instead of a verdict. Even familiar buildings look provisional, as if their stone facades were stage sets waiting for actors.\n\nSoon, traffic will harden into lanes and calendars will begin making demands. Coffee will cool beside keyboards. Elevators will carry small silences between floors. People will hurry beneath clocks they pretend not to obey. Yet some trace of morning\u2019s openness survives. It hides in the decision to hold a door, to call a friend, or to eat lunch outside beneath a tree.\n\nPerhaps freedom is rarely grand. Perhaps it arrives as a handful of unclaimed minutes and asks only to be noticed. The world does not pause for us, but it offers pauses: rain under an awning, music from an open window, sunlight resting on a chipped cup. When I remember this, even an ordinary day feels less like a corridor and more like a field, crossed by paths that appear only when someone chooses to wander slowly.",
